WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.180
Defensive programming means designing code to behave safely when assumptions are wrong.

2
00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:10.120
In TypeScript, this starts with an honest view of uncertainty.

3
00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:18.620
A type may express what a function expects, a caller may promise to pass the right value, and a service may usually return the documented response.

4
00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.880
Defensive code still asks what happens when one of those expectations fails.

5
00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.880
TypeScript prevents many design-time mistakes.

6
00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:31.980
It can catch missing properties in your own code, flag impossible assignments, and make refactors safer.

7
00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:35.600
But TypeScript does not automatically control runtime reality.

8
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:50.500
Values that arrive from users, APIs, queues, files, browser storage, configuration, databases, and third-party libraries may be malformed, missing, stale, hostile, or simply different from what the code expected.

9
00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:55.900
Good defensive TypeScript narrows uncertainty at boundaries instead of spreading it across the codebase.

10
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.760
Raw input should be treated as unknown until it has been checked.

11
00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:07.860
Once the program has validated a value, it can convert that value into a safer internal shape and let the rest of the system depend on clearer assumptions.

12
00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:10.860
The goal is not paranoid code everywhere.

13
00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:15.360
Defensive programming is not a license to bury every line under noisy checks.

14
00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:21.700
The goal is clear assumptions, explicit validation, safe failure, and maintainable behavior.

15
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:28.840
A defensive system shows which inputs are trusted, which invariants are required, and what should happen when something unexpected appears.

16
00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:36.000
This discipline improves reliability, security, debugging, and long-term engineering quality.

17
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:47.780
Defensive code tends to fail closer to the cause of a problem, produce clearer operational signals, and avoid surprising success that later becomes data loss, authorization leakage, or a production incident.

18
00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:54.680
In TypeScript, defensive programming means using both the type system and runtime checks in the places where they each provide real protection.
